From: Onigiri
Subject: [PATCH] Set the wired monitor's brightness over DDC/CI, by hand or from the light sensor

MonitorBrightness starts with DisplayManagerService at boot completed. While a wired monitor is on,
it writes levels to the kernel's secdp_ddc_brightness node, which sends them to the monitor over
DDC/CI. The Monitor brightness page and the DeX panel's slider write Settings.Global
onigiri_monitor_auto and _level. With Auto off the monitor takes the level. With Auto on, the light
sensor beside the screen in use (the inner one while the inner panel is on, else the cover side's
Rear ALS) picks a level on a learned curve: five points spread on a log scale from 2 lux to 2000
lux, 10 to 100 at first, stored in onigiri_monitor_curve. A slider moved with Auto on moves the two
points around the light of the moment so it gives that level, keeping the curve rising, so a change
in the dark leaves daylight alone. The light rises over about 2 s and falls over about 8 s, and Auto
aims only for changes of 2 or more. Every change fades over a second and a half, eased and spaced
evenly in perceived brightness, in steps 170 ms apart (faster writes make monitors flash a wrong
level), and its last level is sent again a second later, as monitors can drop a write. The page and
the DeX panel read sys.onigiri.monitor.level (the level last set, "unsupported" after three failed
writes in a row, empty without a monitor) and sys.onigiri.monitor.lux.

